  "full_text": "Hello and welcome back to JD Science. Today we are exploring homeostasis. Homeostasis means keeping conditions inside the body at the right level. Temperature, ion content, water content and blood glucose are all controlled. Control systems have three parts. Receptors detect a. A change, a coordinator such as the brain processes the information and effectors such as muscles and glands produce the response. Negative feedback means the output of a process controls its own level. For example, when blood glucose rises, insulin is released to lower it. When glucose falls, glucagon is released higher tier. Negative feedback opposes a change from a normal operating range. For example, after a meal, rising blood glucose stimulates pancreatic beta cells to release insulin, promoting glucose uptake and storage. When blood glucose falls, pancreatic alpha cells release glucagon, stimulating the liver to release glucose. The nervous and endocrine systems both contribute to maintaining a stable internal environment. As you revise this topic, try to explain the key process in your own words. Focus on what happens, why it happens and how the structures involved help the process work. Now pause the video and try this exam style question. Describe the role of receptors, coordinators and effectors in a control system. Here is a model answer. Receptors detect a change in the internal or external environment. The coordinator, such as the brain, processes the information. Effectors, such as muscles or glands, bring about a response. If this explanation has helped, please subscribe to JD Science for more lessons. You can find further learning resources and practice questions at jdscience.co.uk. Thank you for watching, Ick.",
